onstrates some of the  kind of classic um influences we had  but also something that feels very new  and very of this time  and that's where the ronin 2 gimbal  comes in  because um you know we had new tools  like that  that was doing just what hal ashby and  um you know  house uh axel vessler were doing back in  the day  but nowadays we have this um technology  that means it's so  it's so much lighter and nimble so it  really and  allowed us on nomad land to to  to kind of maintain a real sort of  elegant  cinematic sensibility yet move really  quick and get the camera inside of these  real and orchestrated uh  situations chloe from your point of view  it's really it's really good to maybe  bring it into into that point  uh how many people were in our company  and how many people  how few people were actually  orchestrating that  shot because it just it's i i kind of  like the idea of just you know  letting the big uh big crews know how  how fast and easy and  nimble a smaller crew can be i would  also add to that as you described it  uh josh the feeling of anxiety i was  getting at my stomach as you redescribed  it  the light all the nomads who've never  done this before and they all each have  a job and they've got to hit it and you  know and all of it and it's just  like the the the wizardry or whatever  little pixie dust was there to  help sort of move that along the gods of  cinema smiled  uh i guess chloe did it feel like a very  special shot  in the moment how did you want to  utilize the whole golden hour  atmosphere we call it the magic hour  hustle  so the crew members months after the  shoot  would tell me so around four o'clock i  started to feel anxious in my stomach  i don't know why but i feel like  something needs to happen so there's a  there's  um it takes a lot of commitment for for  everyone  and and our and our company to take that  leap of faith  every day to say we're going to trust  the people that we're filming we're  going to trust the weather we're going  to trust everything is going to the  equipment  that we're going to wait until the right  time i think josh used to say that um  a magic a sunset is one how  we are supposed to look like as people  or something like well i forgot the  phrase it's like everything just blend  together  us and the world when we others around  us suddenly the edges  dissolve and we all become one and  that's why the sunsets are so important  for so many cultures you know inspire so  many  spirituality in religion it's for that  reason and i think  sometimes with the films that are made  they're people who live in the  peripheral of society  and sometimes they're they're captured  in very harsh journalistic lenses  you know or from news you know it's a  camera with with very  unforgiving and i think it's really  important for us to  to put a cinematic lens in on their in  their lives because that's how they fern  feels when she step out the  her her uh her van and uh that's what  she sees  what she sees is more beautiful than we  can actually ever capture  right but we try our best to do it so  people understand why  they chose that lifestyle molly the  tracking shop  magic hour shot that we all have been  talking about takes place at an event  called the rubber tram rendezvous rtr as  as it's referred to as you referred to  it  can you just tell everyone watching and  listening to this about the importance  of that event  sure yeah i mean a lot of the van  dwellers  it can be a very solitary life and but  there's once  once a year um they're all kind of come  together  to this area in quartzite um where  they share different tips  um but it also is a place of community  too where  um because like i said it could  sometimes be a very solitary life  they cannot they can all be together and  so we were  um we were staging this to be an early  an early rtr  so from so where it only started out  because  like 50 people um and we  and so we staged this to be right before  the real rtr which has now grown to be  i think that year it was 10 000 people  came together  um and so we we gathered these these 50  people in their vehicles and then  you know staged it and they actually  lived you know  they actually kind of created their own  movie rtr because like when we left with  the camera they were all living there  and and so i think that that also added  to this lived in feeling because they  literally were  living there and creating a community  dan from a production point of view was  there something particularly  difficult or challenging about  recreating the  rtr i i certainly think it was the most  challenging  production standpoint that we took on as  a team  um to me i think it speaks to  the magic of the film is the degree to  which you should have no idea what is  staged and what is reality  and i think one of the themes of the  conversation is the degree to which  we go to great lengths for you not to  know that so  rtr stands out as the single  biggest production or art challenge that  we took on in the film  because as molly said we completely  staged it from scratch and the whole  idea was for it to feel like an rtr  i think that there's a lot of practical  considerations to go into that  and to me it's a fascinating part of the  process of working with chloe because  what's amazing about how i think  she makes movies is that it was a  legitimate conversation of do we  actually  shoot at the real rtr and there were a  lot of reasons we didn't  the first one as molly said is story  based because  the current rtr is just too big for when  our story takes place  but i think there's a much broader point  that that connects back to the tracking  shot of  is we actually want to be able to  control  real environments for a cinematic  practice that's not documentary based  and so i think if you combine the  production infrastructure  of staging this huge undertaking which i  think our crew would take  huge pride in with the tracking shot  to me it's kind of emblematic of how  many layers of reality  we're playing with at every moment of  the film and i don't think any of this  has to do with the experience  the viewer should not be thinking about  that but  from a behind-the-scenes perspective i  think it's a fascinating  experience i have one last question for  chloe  can you tell me a little bit about how  the script  may have changed or evolved after you  cast and got to know some of the real  life nomads that are  in the movie how did the script change  to suit them  because they are playing versions of  themselves  yeah i mean the there is one main  um emotional journey for fur  and then and then you know she brunch  out a little bit here and there and  that's where all the other characters  comes in so  so as i meet swanky and then i met with  bob  and then my derrick and every time i  meet someone i get  and then they would tell me a lot about  their lives and then there are certain  parts  of their lives i go oh that i think  that's going to fit into this part of  fern's life  right and i have to i have to tweak what  fern is doing  as well so then so everything is sort of  puzzled together that way  and to me i i don't i think it will  always be this way when i make films i  don't think the script will lock until  last day of shooting um it could be  challenging for  for for the producing team for the cast  but um  yeah i don't know it's uh it's uh it's  the only way i know how to make films so  far  well whatever you all did it worked and  it worked beautifully  and i really appreciate you taking the  time to talk about nomad land  with me for everyone watching so thank  you to  chloe francis mcdormand joshua james  richards  dan janby peter spears molly asher and  thank you all so much  for watching take care thank you bye  bye

